-- APPLIED 2026-10-10 via scripts/run-sql-file.mjs. Not in schema_migrations.
-- 102: budget-room sentences rewritten to the measured budget tier (101: regional research + queue budget-room pass C1-C4, 2026-10-10). Places in C1-C4 were searched for cheaper printed tariffs (dharamshala, rest house) and none were found.


BEGIN;
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl text, col text, key text, old_value text, saved_at timestamptz default now());
INSERT INTO backups.prose_cost_20261010 (tbl, col, key, old_value) VALUES
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/7', $cst$This is when Chitrakoot reveals its landscape credentials. Hanuman Dhara becomes a proper cascade, the view from the top spanning a green Bundelkhand that looks nothing like the dust bowl of April. The boat rides on a swollen Mandakini have genuine drama — the river moves with purpose now. Kamadgiri parikrama in monsoon is muddy and atmospheric: devotees walk the sacred hill through rain, chanting, and the forest smells of renewal. Ram Ghat's steps disappear under rising water, changing the geometry of the evening aarti. Rooms: ₹300-800/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/6', $cst$The transformation is dramatic. Dry waterfalls reactivate within days: Hanuman Dhara runs strong, Ranawari fills its pool, and dozens of unnamed cascades appear across the hills behind Chitrakoot. The forest canopy rebuilds rapidly — Bundelkhand vegetation wastes no time. Ram Ghat receives its first high-water devotees, and the aarti against a swollen, muddy Mandakini has a different power than the dry-season version. Rooms: ₹300-800/night. June is two months in one — avoid the first three weeks, but the last week (if monsoon arrives on schedule) is electrifying.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chittorgarh/7', $cst$Monsoon Chittorgarh Fort is an entirely different monument. Moss creeps up the Vijay Stambha, peacocks display on palace walls, and every Rajput romance novel setting comes alive when you add rain, green, and emptiness. The water bodies fill — some for the first time since last monsoon — and the fort's genius as a self-sustaining citadel becomes tangible. Padmini's Palace, reflected in its restored pool during monsoon, achieves the beauty that launched the legends. Slippery stone paths require careful footwork. Rooms: ₹500-1,200/night. July is when the fort stops being a ruin and remembers it was a city.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'guptkashi/3', $cst$Watch the town transform in real time — by late March, the main road has fresh signboards for Kedarnath helicopter services, guesthouses display tariff cards, and ponywallas start bringing their horses down from winter pastures. The Vishwanath Temple sees small groups of early pilgrims — mainly retired couples from the plains who prefer quiet darshan. The cherry blossoms on the road between Guptkashi and Kalimath are genuine competition for Japan's famous varieties, though nobody has marketed them. The Kalimath Temple, an important Shakti Pith 10 km away, is accessible and uncrowded in March. Rooms are still off-season priced at ₹600-1200. The Saturday market has fresh pahadi greens and local honey. Get your accommodation locked in now for May — the best-located guesthouses with valley views book early and don't list on apps.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rudraprayag/1', $cst$January Rudraprayag rewards the curious transit traveller. The confluence viewing point (10-minute walk from town) shows the Mandakini and Alaknanda meeting in winter clarity — different temperatures, different mineral loads, visibly different colours merging. The Koteshwar Mahadev temple, carved into a riverside cave, is Rudraprayag's hidden gem — in January you'll share it with priests and local devotees only. The Jim Corbett connection is real: the man-eating leopard killed 125 people between 1918-1926, and Corbett's account is one of India's great adventure narratives. Read it before you visit. Hotels are basic (₹600-1,200) but functional. Rudraprayag is 141km from Rishikesh (5-6 hours by road) and works as an overnight stop on any Garhwal exploration.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'rishikesh/10', $cst$October Rishikesh is the platonic ideal. The rafting is at its absolute best: the river has post-monsoon power but post-monsoon predictability, the water temperature is perfect (no wetsuit needed, no warm-bathwater feel), and the rapids — Marine Drive, Roller Coaster, Golf Course — hit with satisfying force. The 16km run takes 3-4 hours and costs ₹1,200-1,800. On the same trip you can add cliff jumping and body surfing in calm sections. Off the river: Beatles Ashram in October light is worth every rupee of the entry fee — the graffiti-covered buildings against green forest and blue sky photograph beautifully. Triveni Ghat aarti is at peak attendance and energy. Neelkanth Temple trek is the best it gets: 25°C, clear trail, visible peaks. Bungee jumping at Jumpin Heights has its longest queues but fastest throughput — book morning for shortest wait. The yoga studios are packed with quality: post-monsoon is when the best international teachers rotate through Rishikesh. Parmarth Niketan evening aarti draws 500+ people; arrive 45 minutes early for a front-row ghat seat. Room rates reflect demand: ₹1,500-3,000, book a week ahead. AIIMS is 15 minutes away. The only complaint about October Rishikesh is that everyone else figured it out too.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'uttarkashi/2', $cst$February continues winter solitude. NIM's basic mountaineering courses (BMC, 26 days, ₹5,000-7,000 — remarkable value for professional instruction) take trainees into the surrounding mountains for ice craft, rope work, and snow survival. Watching NIM trainees practice on the training rock near the campus is free entertainment. The Kashi Vishwanath temple morning aarti (6am) is attended by a handful of devotees — deeply personal. The local market sells Garhwali woolens, dried fruits, and rajma (kidney beans) that the region is famous for. The Bhagirathi gorge walk toward Gangnani hot springs (40km upstream) is cold but beautiful. Hotels at ₹500-1,000. February Uttarkashi is unpolished, cold, and genuine — the mountain town equivalent of a favourite worn sweater.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'champawat/12', $cst$There is something honest about visiting a 12th-century temple complex in December cold. The carvings do not need your comfort to be extraordinary. Morning frost on the Baleshwar temple platform melts slowly under winter sun, each stone carved figure emerging from ice like a revelation on a schedule. The market sells roasted peanuts and gur (jaggery) that taste better when your hands are cold. The road to Tanakpur is clear but the surrounding hills can be icy. Christmas week brings zero visitors — Champawat does not know it is a holiday. Rooms: ₹400-800/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'prayagraj/5', $cst$There is almost no tourist reason to visit Prayagraj in May. The heat is genuinely dangerous — heatstroke cases spike across UP plains cities. The rivers look tired. The city survives on mangoes, sugarcane juice, and air conditioning. If you're passing through by train, the station's waiting rooms are air-conditioned. The only upside: hotel rooms bottom out at ₹500-800 and you can negotiate anything. Civil Lines restaurants keep their AC maxed. This is when Prayagraj lives indoors.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/5', $cst$The honest advice: do not come in May unless you have a specific spiritual commitment. That said, if you are here, the early-morning Kamadgiri parikrama in pre-dawn dark (4:30-6:30am, carry a torch) has a mystical quality that cooler months lack — you walk the sacred hill with only the most dedicated devotees. The caves provide genuine relief. And the evening aarti at Ram Ghat, when the day's heat finally breaks, brings a collective exhale from the town that is itself a spiritual experience. Rooms: ₹300-800/night with widespread vacancy.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/4', $cst$Ram Navami in Chitrakoot is as close to the source as this festival gets — celebrating Ram's birthday at the place where he spent his exile. The temples fill, the ghats come alive, and for a few days the heat is irrelevant because devotion provides its own energy. Outside the festival, April visits require discipline: 5-9am, then shelter until 4pm. The caves and forest walks are cooler than open areas. Gupt Godavari's underground stream feels air-conditioned by comparison. The boatmen on the Mandakini still operate early mornings, and a river ride past Ram Ghat as dawn breaks is worth the 4:30am alarm. Rooms: ₹300-1,000/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chittorgarh/6', $cst$Post-first-rain Chittorgarh Fort is a water engineering showcase. Every surface is designed to channel monsoon water: into the Gaumukh reservoir, into the palace cisterns, into the 84 water bodies within the fort walls. Watching a 700-acre hilltop fortress manage a deluge using medieval hydraulics is more impressive than most modern infrastructure. The stone darkens, the Vijay Stambha reflects in puddles, and the heat breaks like a fever. Pre-monsoon June, however, is the worst time to visit anywhere in Rajasthan. Rooms: ₹400-1,000/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'champawat/7', $cst$Champawat in July is for the genuinely curious. The Bhimeshwara Mahadev temple — less famous than Baleshwar but arguably more atmospheric — sits in a grove that becomes a green cathedral in monsoon. Waterfalls appear on the hillside above the Nagnath temple that do not exist in any other month. Local life continues: school operates, markets function, chai flows. The isolation can feel profound or oppressive depending on your temperament. Roads to Abbott Mount are risky, but in-town exploration is fine. Rooms: ₹400-800/night with near-zero occupancy.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chittorgarh/5', $cst$The honest advice: come another month. If here, the fort at 5:30am — before the stone absorbs the day's heat — has an emptiness that is its own kind of experience. You will share 700 acres with maybe three other humans and a population of langurs. The Rana Kumbha palace ruins in early light, with nobody else present, feel like a personal archaeological discovery. The town's lassi shops become essential infrastructure — mango lassi in May is not a beverage, it is medicine. Rooms: ₹500-1,200/night. May Chittorgarh is for masochists and misanthropes. Both will be satisfied.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'solan/3', $cst$March Solan in spring bloom — cherry and plum blossoms in the orchards around the town — has a gentle beauty that doesn't make postcards but makes memories. The Shoolini Devi temple spring aarti draws local devotees. The heritage railway through blossoming hillsides is charming. The Chambaghat mushroom research centre is accessible and informative — India's mushroom industry was basically pioneered from this town. The brewery complex in spring is walkable. Day trips to Kasauli (spring flowers, colonial architecture) and the Barog railway halt (chai at a station that hasn't changed in a century) flesh out the stay. Hotels at ₹800-1,500. March Solan is for people who find Shimla exhausting and want the Himachal hill experience with honesty instead of hype.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'uttarkashi/1', $cst$January Uttarkashi reveals itself without the Char Dham filter. The Kashi Vishwanath temple — ancient, riverside, housing one of the 12 jyotirlingas according to some traditions — is peaceful in winter. The NIM campus (founded 1965 by Jawaharlal Nehru, India's premier mountaineering training institute) is worth visiting: the museum of mountaineering equipment, expedition photographs, and Himalayan history is excellent. The Bhagirathi (the river that becomes the Ganga at Devprayag) runs clear and cold. The Maneri Dam (upstream) creates a reservoir used for winter kayaking by NIM trainees. Hotels at ₹400-800 are basic and warm. The drive from Rishikesh (155km, 5-6 hours) through Tehri and Chamba is scenic Garhwal. January Uttarkashi is for mountaineering enthusiasts, temple devotees, and Garhwal explorers who want a functional mountain town without pilgrim-season chaos.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'solan/11', $cst$November Solan is autumn quiet. The orchards turn colour and drop leaves. The heritage railway through autumn forest is charming. The town market shifts to winter foods — roasted chestnuts, local woolens, dried fruit. The Mohan Meakin brewery in autumn light has a particular character. The Shoolini temple is peaceful. Hotels at ₹600-1,200. Kasauli in November is lovely. November Solan is for people who don't need a hill station to perform — they just need it to exist, comfortably and honestly.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'solan/12', $cst$December Solan is cold, foggy, and the brewery still makes rum. The heritage railway in winter fog, emerging from the Barog tunnel into cold valley air, is atmospheric. The mushroom farms — climate-controlled, indifferent to weather — continue production. The town market sells winter warmth: local honey, pickles, woolens. Christmas is a small affair. New Year's passes without fuss. Hotels at ₹500-1,000. Solan in December is where you stay if you want to be in the Himachal hills for the winter atmosphere without the Shimla premium or the high-altitude extremes. It's practical, warm enough indoors, and it makes no false promises.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'solan/9', $cst$September Solan clearing from monsoon has a fresh-washed quality. The hills are green, the air is clean, and the town resumes normal operations. The heritage railway in September — green forest, occasional rain, clear patches — is pleasant. The orchards start harvest season. The mushroom farms continue. Late September is a genuinely good time for Solan as a Shimla alternative: uncrowded, mild (16-26°C), and honest. The Kasauli and Dagshai day trips are comfortable. Hotels at ₹600-1,200 with easy availability.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'manali/11', $cst$The appeal is extremely niche. If you want to experience a mountain town in hibernation mode — near-empty streets, ₹500-700 rooms, fireplace-lit guesthouses with maybe two other guests — November delivers that. Vashisht hot springs feel wonderful in the cold. The handful of Old Manali cafes that stay open (Lazy Dog, Drifters, a few others) have a small, committed community of long-stay travellers. Hadimba Temple without crowds is peaceful, surrounded by bare deodars. The Atal Tunnel remains open, and Lahaul Valley has a stark, lunar beauty in November — Keylong and Udaipur are accessible if desolate. But there's no sugar-coating it: there's very little to do. No trekking (trails are cold and exposed), no rafting (Beas too low), no paragliding (operators closed), no Rohtang. If the first snowfall arrives in late November, it transforms everything overnight — but betting your holiday on a weather event is not a plan.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chittorgarh/8', $cst$Teej celebrations bring Chittorgarh's women out in finest Rajasthani dress — green bangles, leheriya sarees, and swings decorated with flowers. The festival, celebrating Parvati's union with Shiva, has a feminine joy that contrasts powerfully with the fort's martial history. The Vijay Stambha, Kirti Stambha, and the three jauhar sites are lush, almost softened by monsoon green. August light through rain clouds creates dramatic photographs of the fort silhouette. Evening light-and-sound show operates if rain permits. Rooms: ₹500-1,200/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_payoff', 'chitrakoot/9', $cst$This is Chitrakoot's most underrated month. The waterfalls still flow, the forest is at peak green, the temples are uncrowded, and the weather cooperates. The Kamadgiri parikrama is beautiful: the sacred hill wears green like ceremonial dress, and morning light through clearing monsoon clouds creates theatrical effects. The boat ride on a full Mandakini, past painted ghats and bathing devotees, is the postcard image — except you are in it, not looking at it. Sati Anusuya forest trail is lush and leech-free by late September. Rooms: ₹400-1,000/night.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_lead', 'solan/5', $cst$May brings summer visitors — 18-30°C. Solan offers what Shimla increasingly can't: quiet. The town's modest infrastructure means modest crowds. The mushroom farms at Chambaghat offer an unusual agri-tourism experience. The heritage railway is in full swing. Hotels fill but prices stay reasonable (₹800-2,000). The evenings cool to 18°C — pleasant walks around the town.$cst$),
('destination_months', 'prose_lead', 'solan/11', $cst$November cools Solan — 5-18°C. The town settles into winter approach. The orchards drop leaves. The mushroom farms continue (climate-controlled, year-round). The Mohan Meakin brewery is atmospheric in autumn light. Tourist pressure is minimal. Hotels at ₹600-1,200. The heritage railway through autumn forest is charming. Solan is what it always is: quiet, functional, and honest.$cst$);
